---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ExcelToCsv from '@/components/tools/data/ExcelToCsv.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="excel-to-csv">
  <ExcelToCsv client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert Excel to CSV</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the workbook onto the page. <code>.xlsx</code>, <code>.xls</code>, <code>.xlsm</code>, <code>.xlsb</code> and <code>.ods</code> all work.</li>
      <li>Choose the worksheet. The tool lists all sheets in the file, and the preview shows the first twelve rows so you can confirm your choice.</li>
      <li>Set the delimiter and date format: comma for most systems, semicolon for European Excel, tab for a TSV.</li>
      <li>Download. Save the current sheet as one CSV, or get all sheets at once as a ZIP with one file per worksheet.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What survives the conversion, and what cannot</h2>
    <p>
      CSV is a plain grid of text. That is its strength and its limit. Know what will be lost before you send
      the file to a colleague.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-max border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">Workbook feature</th>
            <th class="border-b px-3 py-2 text-left font-semibold">In the CSV</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Cell values and text</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Kept</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Formulas</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Replaced by their last calculated result</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Number formats (currency, percent)</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Your choice: raw value or the displayed text</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Dates</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Kept, in the format you select</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Merged cells</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Value in the top-left cell; the rest empty</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Multiple worksheets</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">One CSV each; download them together as a ZIP</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Colours, fonts, borders, column widths</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Not representable in CSV</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Charts, images, pivot tables, macros</td><td class="border-b px-3 py-2">Not representable in CSV</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Raw values versus displayed text</h2>
    <p>
      A cell that shows <code>£1,234.50</code> holds the number <code>1234.5</code> with a currency format.
      The correct value for your CSV depends on where it goes:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep <strong>Use underlying numbers</strong> on (the default) when a database, script or BI tool reads the file. You get <code>1234.5</code>, which all systems can parse, with no symbols or thousands separators.</li>
      <li>Turn it off when a person reads the file, or when you must reproduce a report exactly as it looked on screen. You get <code>£1,234.50</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Getting dates right</h2>
    <p>
      Excel stores a date as the number of days since 30 December 1899, and the format controls how it looks.
      Export “as displayed” keeps what you saw, but <code>03/04/2026</code> is ambiguous: the third of April in
      London and the fourth of March in New York. If the CSV goes into an automated system, choose ISO 8601
      (<code>2026-08-16</code>): it sorts correctly as text, has no locale ambiguity, and all databases accept
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the browser is the right place for this</h2>
    <p>
      Spreadsheets often hold salary bands, patient lists and unreleased financials. A conversion site gets a
      copy, and many keep the file for hours. Here SheetJS decodes the workbook in your tab, and no request
      carries the data. The only limit on file size is the memory of your machine, and the tool works with the
      network turned off.
    </p>

    <h2>Related conversions</h2>
    <p>
      For structured output, <a href="/excel-to-json">Excel to JSON</a> keeps types and nesting. In the other
      direction, <a href="/csv-to-excel">CSV to Excel</a> writes a real workbook with auto-fitted columns.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
